<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alban Hills</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Colli Albani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alban Hills are the remains of an extinct volcano about 20 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with crater lakes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albano">Lake Albano</a> and Lake Nemi. In Roman tradition they were the site of Alba Longa, the mother city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>, and of the Latin festival on the Alban Mount. Towns such as Tusculum stood on their slopes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alban-hills.jpg" alt="Alban Hills" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NikonZ7II · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Cavo_Visto_da_castel_gandolfo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
